-- title:
--   The Lie algebra $L$ generated by $\mathsf H$
-- statement:
--   The Lie algebra generated by $\mathsf H$ is the smallest set of $n$-qubit operators containing $\mathsf H$ and closed under real scalar multiplication, addition and the commutator $(A,B)\mapsto i[A,B]$ (the "allowed composition operations for a Lie algebra" of Appendix B). Equivalently, after multiplying by $i$, it is
--   $$
--   L=\operatorname{Lie}_{\mathbb R}\{\,iG : G\in\mathsf H\,\},
--   $$
--   the smallest real Lie subalgebra of $n$-qubit matrices, with bracket $[A,B]=AB-BA$, containing $iG$ for every generator $G$. A Hamiltonian $X$ is generated by $\mathsf H$ exactly when $iX\in L$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The map $X\mapsto iX$ turns the book's Hermitian algebra with bracket $i[\cdot,\cdot]$ into the anti-Hermitian algebra $L$, since $i\,(i[A,B])=[iA,iB]$. Only real scalars are allowed and $iI$ is not a generator.
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, Theorem 9.2.1, p.127 (PDF p.144); Appendix B, p.234 (PDF p.251)

import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_WeakUniv_gens

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

namespace BaconThesis.WeakUniv

/-- **The Lie algebra `L` generated by `H`.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1,
Theorem 9.2.1, p.127 (PDF p.144): "If L denotes the Lie algebra generated by H"; Appendix B,
p.234 (PDF p.251): H gives rise to a Lie algebra "via scalar multiplication, addition, and
Lie-commutator; i.e. the allowed composition operations for a Lie algebra".

`lieAlg n` is the smallest real Lie subalgebra of `Matrix (Fin n → Fin 2) (Fin n → Fin 2) ℂ`
(bracket `[A, B] = AB − BA`, Mathlib's `LieRing.ofAssociativeRing`, a local instance in Mathlib and
in every file that uses `lieAlg`) containing `i·G` for every generator `G ∈ gens n`.

**Formalization Note.** The book works with Hermitian Hamiltonians, real scalars and the
Hermitian bracket `i[A, B]` (as in (B.3)–(B.4)). The map `H ↦ iH` is a Lie algebra isomorphism from
that algebra onto the real Lie span of `{iG : G ∈ H}` used here (`i·(i[A, B]) = [iA, iB]`), so a
Hamiltonian `X` is generated by `H` exactly when `i • X ∈ lieAlg n`, and `su(d)` corresponds to
traceless anti-Hermitian matrices. Only real scalars are allowed, and `iI` is not a generator. -/
noncomputable def lieAlg (n : ℕ) : LieSubalgebra ℝ (Matrix (Fin n → Fin 2) (Fin n → Fin 2) ℂ) :=
  LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ _ ((fun G => Complex.I • G) '' gens n)

end BaconThesis.WeakUniv


